    {
      "id": "f25338c3-6351-437d-b3ba-b3a95f80af88",
      "name": "Why MCP is awesome: tools that actually fit the job",
      "path": "WWW / Blog / Why MCP is awesome: tools that actually fit the job",
      "docTypeId": "218d5888-d9a4-456e-a461-c99996999dd4",
      "templateId": "1020b04f-26c7-4e49-95c1-7f65eb0480c9",
      "state": "Published",
      "domains": [],
      "values": [
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextBox",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "title",
          "value": "Why MCP is awesome: tools that actually fit the job"
        },
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.DateTime",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "publishDate",
          "value": "2026-10-01 09:00:00"
        },
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextArea",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "excerpt",
          "value": "The Model Context Protocol turns a chat assistant into something that can actually do the work — here is what that feels like when you build a whole site through it."
        },
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.MediaPicker3",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "listingImage",
          "value": [
            {
              "key": "11111111-1111-4111-8111-111111111111",
              "mediaKey": "c4bef375-bb4a-49a6-8d33-289b0ad00438",
              "mediaTypeAlias": "Image",
              "crops": [],
              "focalPoint": null
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.BlockGrid",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "body",
          "value": {
            "layout": {
              "Umbraco.BlockGrid": [
                {
                  "$type": "BlockGridLayoutItem",
                  "columnSpan": 12,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                  "areas": [],
                  "contentKey": "2919cad4-c01c-4c3a-9cf6-564d86c877f3",
                  "settingsKey": null
                },
                {
                  "$type": "BlockGridLayoutItem",
                  "columnSpan": 12,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                  "areas": [],
                  "contentKey": "d4c802bb-7aa7-4030-82c8-1324f92d1170",
                  "settingsKey": null
                },
                {
                  "$type": "BlockGridLayoutItem",
                  "columnSpan": 12,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                  "areas": [],
                  "contentKey": "54d1e2b5-bbfe-4a66-bf18-34bdb401b946",
                  "settingsKey": null
                },
                {
                  "$type": "BlockGridLayoutItem",
                  "columnSpan": 12,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                  "areas": [],
                  "contentKey": "8d8c491f-fa9a-4066-9ddf-d561ac45b3d0",
                  "settingsKey": null
                }
              ]
            },
            "contentData": [
              {
                "contentTypeKey": "dce1cdce-24c9-482d-85d7-65df7740fd6d",
                "key": "2919cad4-c01c-4c3a-9cf6-564d86c877f3",
                "values": [
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.RichText",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "text",
                    "value": {
                      "markup": "<p>The Model Context Protocol (MCP) is the reason this very site exists. Not a theme, not a plugin — a whole content model, pages, images and all, assembled by an agent talking to Umbraco over MCP. Before MCP, an assistant could only talk; with MCP it can <em>do</em>: create document types, publish pages, upload media, assign domains. The tools are not bolted on afterwards — they are the interface.</p><p>What makes MCP elegant is that one protocol works everywhere. The Umbraco MCP server exposes the CMS's own management API as tools; an editor MCP server does the same for content. One agent, two servers, zero custom glue code. The model never scrapes a backoffice or guesses at an endpoint — it calls a named tool with typed arguments and gets structured results back.</p>",
                      "blocks": {
                        "layout": {},
                        "contentData": [],
                        "settingsData": [],
                        "expose": []
                      }
                    }
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "contentTypeKey": "c4a10c07-5c15-4daa-b3f3-6025b22c3bb9",
                "key": "d4c802bb-7aa7-4030-82c8-1324f92d1170",
                "values": [
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.MediaPicker3",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "image",
                    "value": [
                      {
                        "key": "22222222-2222-4222-8222-222222222222",
                        "mediaKey": "862d1b32-082e-464b-949e-eed95fa9ae84",
                        "mediaTypeAlias": "Image",
                        "crops": [],
                        "focalPoint": null
                      }
                    ]
                  },
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextBox",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "caption",
                    "value": "A network of lines and dots — every MCP tool is a node the model can reach. Photo: Conny Schneider on Unsplash."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "contentTypeKey": "dce1cdce-24c9-482d-85d7-65df7740fd6d",
                "key": "54d1e2b5-bbfe-4a66-bf18-34bdb401b946",
                "values": [
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.RichText",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "text",
                    "value": {
                      "markup": "<p>There is a quiet benefit to tool-driven work: every step is verifiable. Each call returns a real id, and each id can be read back. When a schema write lands, you fetch the document type and confirm the properties; when a page is created, you fetch the page and check the values. Nothing is taken on faith, and nothing fails quietly — a bad payload comes back as an error that says exactly what to fix.</p><p>It also keeps the architecture honest. Because the agent must go through Umbraco's own APIs, the conventions of the platform — document type folders, compositions, block grids — are enforced by the CMS itself rather than by prompt instructions. The result is a site that looks exactly like one built by hand in the backoffice, because in every way that matters, it was.</p>",
                      "blocks": {
                        "layout": {},
                        "contentData": [],
                        "settingsData": [],
                        "expose": []
                      }
                    }
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "contentTypeKey": "1613422d-8e25-40c5-80f2-fc16b771709a",
                "key": "8d8c491f-fa9a-4066-9ddf-d561ac45b3d0",
                "values": [
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextArea",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "quote",
                    "value": "MCP turns \"please update the site\" from a hope into a workflow: named tools, typed arguments, verifiable results."
                  },
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextBox",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "attribution",
                    "value": "— the whole reason this blog exists"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ],
            "settingsData": [],
            "expose": [
              {
                "contentKey": "2919cad4-c01c-4c3a-9cf6-564d86c877f3",
                "culture": null,
                "segment": null
              },
              {
                "contentKey": "d4c802bb-7aa7-4030-82c8-1324f92d1170",
                "culture": null,
